// An ALIASED type import, and nothing else unusual — the name it aliases is
// unique, so there is no collision to blame.
//
// This program CHECKS CLEAN and TRAPS at run time on the interpreter:
//
//     Shout.shout: no implementation for type 'Doodad'
//
// The interpreter's dispatch key is the value's runtime type NAME, and an
// aliased import puts the ALIAS there, so no impl is registered under it. That
// is a name deciding a lookup.
//
// It is kept as a fixture rather than fixed here because it is a REFERENCE
// defect, and because this builder keys on a `*rt.TypeID` address with no name
// in the path — so a lowered program would answer where the interpreter
// traps, and being more correct than the reference is a DIFF. See
// TestDict_AnAliasedTypeImportBreaksInterpreterDispatch, which fails when the
// interpreter stops mis-keying and is therefore the gate on admitting this.
import {
    std/io
    gadget.{Gadget as Doodad, Shout}
}

fn main() {
    io.print(Shout.shout(Doodad{n: 3}))
}
